WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.589
Voice AI agents can now answer

00:00:02.508 --> 00:00:04.110
calls and take action:

00:00:04.655 --> 00:00:05.677
look up an order,

00:00:05.642 --> 00:00:06.780
change a payout,

00:00:06.722 --> 00:00:07.953
issue a refund.

00:00:08.243 --> 00:00:10.112
But callers can pressure or trick

00:00:10.019 --> 00:00:12.352
them. The question isn't how smart

00:00:12.249 --> 00:00:13.525
the agent sounds.

00:00:13.618 --> 00:00:15.418
It's what stands between its words

00:00:15.314 --> 00:00:16.485
and your money.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.099
BeyondFlow is a voice support agent

00:00:18.996 --> 00:00:20.864
for a sandbox treasury team.

00:00:21.411 --> 00:00:23.698
ElevenLabs gives it a natural voice.

00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:25.939
ArmorIQ decides what it's allowed to

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:28.016
do. Let's see how it works,

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:28.991
then how to set it up

00:00:28.899 --> 00:00:29.920
yourself.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:31.833
Here's the big picture.

00:00:31.858 --> 00:00:33.634
A caller talks to an ElevenLabs

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:35.827
voice agent. When the agent needs

00:00:35.713 --> 00:00:37.628
to act, it calls our gateway.

00:00:37.802 --> 00:00:40.204
The gateway asks ArmorIQ to approve

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:41.899
the action, and only an approved

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:43.908
call reaches our MCP server,

00:00:43.817 --> 00:00:45.174
which holds the tools.

00:00:45.408 --> 00:00:46.788
Three things can happen.

00:00:46.811 --> 00:00:48.936
Allowed: the answer comes back.

00:00:49.227 --> 00:00:50.955
Blocked: the call stops and the

00:00:50.841 --> 00:00:53.023
agent explains. Held:

00:00:53.024 --> 00:00:54.788
the call waits for two supervisors

00:00:54.683 --> 00:00:55.773
to approve.

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.250
ElevenLabs serves as the voice interface.

00:00:59.542 --> 00:01:01.293
Our agent runs directly on its

00:01:01.189 --> 00:01:03.580
conversational platform, and each tool is

00:01:03.488 --> 00:01:05.648
configured as a webhook pointing at

00:01:05.543 --> 00:01:08.213
our gateway, transforming a spoken request

00:01:08.121 --> 00:01:09.606
into a structured call.

00:01:09.897 --> 00:01:11.638
The webhook expects an answer in

00:01:11.522 --> 00:01:12.858
about five seconds,

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:14.425
so when a refund is held,

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.248
the gateway replies immediately with a

00:01:16.156 --> 00:01:18.175
holding utterance, and the caller can

00:01:18.105 --> 00:01:20.346
ask for the decision once supervisors

00:01:20.254 --> 00:01:21.170
review.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.064
ArmorIQ provides the cryptographic control layer.

00:01:25.355 --> 00:01:27.328
We registered our agent and MCP

00:01:27.352 --> 00:01:29.755
servers on the ArmorIQ platform and

00:01:29.674 --> 00:01:31.740
configured three strict policies:

00:01:31.914 --> 00:01:34.189
allow reads, block payout changes,

00:01:34.213 --> 00:01:36.442
and hold refunds for two approvals.

00:01:36.802 --> 00:01:38.764
In code, the gateway uses the

00:01:38.706 --> 00:01:42.270
ArmorIQ SDK in three ordered steps:

00:01:42.504 --> 00:01:43.640
capture the plan,

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:45.057
get an intent token,

00:01:45.057 --> 00:01:47.320
and invoke the tool through ArmorIQ's

00:01:47.228 --> 00:01:49.945
proxy. No allow or block decision

00:01:49.911 --> 00:01:51.373
lives in our own code;

00:01:51.466 --> 00:01:54.519
every verdict comes directly from ArmorIQ.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:56.788
Now, here is how to set

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:58.367
it up yourself from a clean

00:01:58.275 --> 00:02:01.258
repository checkout. Step one:

00:02:01.385 --> 00:02:03.881
create an ArmorIQ account and generate

00:02:03.766 --> 00:02:06.029
an API key on platform dot

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.736
armoriq dot ai.

00:02:08.097 --> 00:02:10.383
Step two: onboard your agent and

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.310
your MCP server on the platform

00:02:12.229 --> 00:02:14.504
dashboard. Step three:

00:02:14.528 --> 00:02:16.200
create the three policies:

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.753
allow reads, block payout changes,

00:02:18.697 --> 00:02:20.902
and hold refunds for two approvals.

00:02:21.193 --> 00:02:24.257
Step four: copy dot env dot

00:02:24.164 --> 00:02:26.126
example to dot env,

00:02:26.185 --> 00:02:27.798
set your API key,

00:02:27.764 --> 00:02:29.689
and start the MCP server and

00:02:29.610 --> 00:02:31.790
gateway with npm run dev.

00:02:32.245 --> 00:02:34.334
Step five: in ElevenLabs,

00:02:34.241 --> 00:02:36.284
create your voice agent and add

00:02:36.216 --> 00:02:38.106
each tool as a webhook pointing

00:02:38.003 --> 00:02:38.966
to the gateway.

00:02:39.326 --> 00:02:42.043
Step six: run npm run test

00:02:41.962 --> 00:02:44.284
colon enforcement and confirm you see

00:02:44.168 --> 00:02:46.106
allowed, blocked, and held.

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.402
Step seven: open the supervisor playground,

00:02:49.578 --> 00:02:50.611
make a test call,

00:02:50.484 --> 00:02:52.177
and approve the held refund with

00:02:52.074 --> 00:02:53.513
two supervisors.

00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:56.332
Here is the boundary test recorded

00:02:56.217 --> 00:02:56.972
from a live,

00:02:56.867 --> 00:02:59.956
real run. Asking 'Where's my order?'

00:03:00.502 --> 00:03:02.870
Allowed: order details returned.

00:03:02.894 --> 00:03:04.449
Asking to send payouts to a

00:03:04.344 --> 00:03:05.493
new routing number?

00:03:05.669 --> 00:03:07.026
Blocked by policy:

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.965
wire diversion prevented.

00:03:09.325 --> 00:03:10.765
Requesting a refund?

00:03:11.056 --> 00:03:12.425
Held under delegation,

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:14.944
until two human supervisors submit approval,

00:03:14.909 --> 00:03:17.127
then released. Same agent,

00:03:17.069 --> 00:03:18.346
same natural voice,

00:03:18.347 --> 00:03:19.831
three distinct outcomes,

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.932
each cryptographically decided by ArmorIQ.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.051
Typical industry safeguards check what is

00:03:25.936 --> 00:03:28.501
said, grant the same static permissions

00:03:28.398 --> 00:03:29.534
for every task,

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:31.253
log what happened afterwards,

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:32.878
or ask a human to approve

00:03:32.775 --> 00:03:35.873
everything. Intent enforcement checks the action

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:37.545
itself, before it runs,

00:03:37.569 --> 00:03:39.693
against a declared plan and policy,

00:03:39.659 --> 00:03:41.818
and brings in human supervisors only

00:03:41.701 --> 00:03:45.034
when high-risk actions demand step-up approval.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.725
BeyondFlow operates as an open-source prototype

00:03:48.621 --> 00:03:50.490
on sandbox treasury data,

00:03:50.665 --> 00:03:52.614
serving as a critical security layer

00:03:52.522 --> 00:03:54.716
among several. BeyondFlow,

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.235
powered by ElevenLabs and ArmorIQ.
